    "turn_text": "just got tremendous background. I know him personally. of many when I was in Congress, he actually came to New Mexico on behalf at one point. And so I watched what he speaks about, and he's talking about the rare earth minerals, and it's a dilemma for Trump, for China, and possibly an opportunity for New Mexico. Years ago, New Mexico had 11 rarer mineral mines just in the second district of, and that's the southern part of the state. Those were all closed down by environmentalists who kept pushing and saying we shouldn't be mining, shouldn't be and so eventually China ended up with all of the jobs, but worse than that, they ended up with the rare earth minerals themselves. Now, these are things that go just about in anything you can imagine, electronic fire, jet parts, cell phones, anything that we use that's electronic has the rare earth minerals in there. They occur in nature in very sparse amounts, and that's a reason they're called rare earth. And the Mexico was a home for those. And so we outsourced those to China. Then China began to add excise taxes on those up to 35%. They were trying to drive our manufacturers of the high-tech military equipment out of business. And so now that Trump is really pushing them, Dick Morris is pointing out that they dominate the market. And they have the capability to shut down our manufacturers of our own military weapons systems if they choose to restrict the supply. So, New Mexico has the opportunity to step into that gap and so recently made a",
